{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A Prayer\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u10022? \u160? \u10022? \u160? \u10022?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8220"Fika Nkosi la ngingafikanga khona mina.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lord, reach beyond the limits of my prayer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8212- Mr E Mkhize (Mkhulu Mkhize, in prayer)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Foreword\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u10022? \u160? \u10022? \u160? \u10022?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before we had language for it, we felt it. Before theology named it and philosophers debated it and scientists dismissed it, something inside every human being has always known that there is more to a person than flesh and bone. That knowing has a name. We call it the soul.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Throughout human history, across every culture and tradition, one question has refused to leave us. Not the question of how the stars were formed. Not whether God exists. Those are profound questions, but they are questions about things outside of us. The question that follows us into our darkest nights, that surfaces at our most honest moments and whispers beneath every loss we have ever suffered, is more intimate than any of those. It is the question we ask not about the universe but about ourselves.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is the soul? And what happens to it?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is the question asked by the Egyptian priest preparing a body for burial, and by the mother sitting beside a grave that should never have been dug. It is the question of the philosopher in his study and the prisoner in his cell. It is the question behind every religion ever founded, every temple ever built, every prayer ever whispered in the dark by someone who was not sure anyone was listening but could not stop themselves from speaking anyway.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 No generation has answered it finally. No tradition has silenced it completely. And perhaps that is the point. Perhaps the question of the soul is not meant to be closed. It is meant to be lived, carried, wrestled with, and allowed to deepen us in the carrying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This book does not claim to have the final answer. It is an invitation. A gentle walk through the spiritual landscape of what our ancestors called the soul, and the many conditions in which a soul might find itself over the course of a lifetime, or even across multiple lifetimes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It was not written from a pulpit or from a place of arrival. It was written from the wilderness. From the long nights, the unanswered prayers, the rejected applications, the seasons where God seemed silent and life seemed impossible. If it carries any authority at all, it is the authority of someone who has lived what is written here and who still, somehow, stubbornly, chooses to believe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 My grandfather used to close every prayer with the words: Fika Nkosi la ngingafikanga khona mina. Lord, reach beyond the limits of my prayer. That prayer is woven into every page of this book. May it reach where I could not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8212- Halalisani Ngema Johannesburg, 2026\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Introduction\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u10022? \u160? \u10022? \u160? \u10022?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 \u8220"Nothing is wrong with you. You are just not where you were meant to grow. Yet.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \u8220"Nothing is wrong with you. You are just not where you were meant to grow. Yet.\u8221"\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If you have ever prayed with everything you had and heard nothing back, at least not in the way you expected, this book was written for you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It was written for the person who has done everything right and still found themselves in the wrong place. For the person who has been faithful, tried hard, loved genuinely, and still ended up on the losing side of too many situations to count. For the person sitting in a season so dark and so long that they have begun to wonder whether light is even real.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This book will not tell you that if you pray harder, give more, or believe better, everything will be fixed. That is not the book you are holding. What this book will tell you, and show you chapter by chapter, is that your soul is not broken. It is not cursed. It is not abandoned. It is in a process that has a name, a shape, and a destination.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The word Renaissance in the title is intentional. Renaissance means rebirth. A coming back to life after a period of darkness or dormancy. The Renaissance in history was preceded by centuries of struggle. And then, out of that long difficult night, came an explosion of art, thought, beauty, and human flourishing that changed the world forever. That is the promise held out to the poor soul. Not that the darkness will be avoided. But that it will not have the last word.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This book follows a journey. It begins with displacement, the sense of being in the wrong place, at the wrong time, overlooked by everyone except God. It moves through confrontation, the raw, honest reckoning with what life has been. It passes through cracking open, the painful moment when the soul stops pretending. It arrives at reframing, the slow discovery that the road has always been taking you somewhere. And it ends in rest. Not the rest of giving up, but the rest of a soul that has finally found its footing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each chapter stands alone. You can read this book from cover to cover or open it to wherever your soul lands. Either way, something here was written for you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Read this book as you would receive a conversation from someone who has been where you are and lived to tell about it. Not a preacher. Not a professor. A fellow traveller. Bruised, still walking, still believing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not a book of answers. It is a book of honest company.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Your renaissance is not behind you. It is ahead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is not a book of answers. It is a book of honest company.\par}
